WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:30.405
Manufacturing costs for a Starlink Terminal fell from $3,000 to below $600. This is First Principles Daily, episode 173. Phase D'Array and Tena's once reached homes only through military contracts priced in the tens of thousands. SpaceX set out to put one on every subscriber's roof at consumer scale, and the path there was a change in how the radio was built, not a change in what the radio does. Defense contractors built phased arrays by assembling hundreds of discrete transmit-received

00:00:30.405 --> 00:00:35.665
modules. Each module held its own amplifiers, phase shifters, and control circuits.

00:00:36.205 --> 00:00:41.305
Volume stayed low, so every module was hand-tuned and tested to military specifications.

00:00:41.945 --> 00:00:46.505
The same small-batch methods and ruggedized parts carried over from radar programs,

00:00:46.905 --> 00:00:51.545
and governments accepted the bill. Nobody questioned the part count or the supply chain.

00:00:52.045 --> 00:00:56.885
A typical legacy array therefore held far more connectors, cables, and enclosures than

00:00:56.885 --> 00:01:03.005
beam steering itself required. SpaceX, vice-president Jonathan Hofeller later described that starting

00:01:03.005 --> 00:01:08.045
build cost as the inherited assumption that electronic beam steering was a low volume,

00:01:08.345 --> 00:01:12.825
high reliability specialty. The industry had never been pressed to treat the antenna

00:01:12.825 --> 00:01:18.705
as a television scale product. Even commercial satellite terminals kept the module by module build

00:01:18.705 --> 00:01:23.925
because the design teams came from the defense side and reused the old qualification processes.

00:01:23.925 --> 00:01:27.645
Connectors between modules added mass and failure points.

00:01:28.065 --> 00:01:32.205
Those interfaces stayed because changing them meant re-qualifying the whole assembly

00:01:32.205 --> 00:01:33.945
under existing procurement rules.

00:01:34.525 --> 00:01:40.005
The finished price was assembly labor, repeated testing, and low volume sourcing, far more

00:01:40.005 --> 00:01:41.965
than the radio frequency physics underneath.

00:01:42.972 --> 00:01:48.332
SpaceX engineers started from what the antenna must physically do, generate and steer a beam

00:01:48.332 --> 00:01:51.212
with radio frequency energy across a flat panel.

00:01:51.212 --> 00:01:56.512
The magic wand number would be the cost of the silicon, the copper, and the laminates

00:01:56.512 --> 00:02:00.132
alone if the atoms could be arranged into a finished terminal for free.

00:02:00.632 --> 00:02:05.652
The sources do not carry those commodity prices, so there is no floor to add up and no

00:02:05.652 --> 00:02:07.292
finished price to divide by it.

00:02:07.752 --> 00:02:10.432
The first principles ratio cannot be derived here.

00:02:10.972 --> 00:02:14.392
What can be walked is the part count and the sequence that made the terminal.

00:02:14.392 --> 00:02:20.932
ST Microelectronics co-designed by CMOS chips that pulled radio frequency functions previously

00:02:20.932 --> 00:02:24.272
spread across several discrete devices onto single dies.

00:02:24.852 --> 00:02:29.592
More than 5 billion of those chips have shipped since the collaboration began around 2015.

00:02:30.432 --> 00:02:35.352
Panel level packaging replaced individual chip scale packages and allowed daily deliveries

00:02:35.352 --> 00:02:36.952
exceeding 5 million units.

00:02:37.592 --> 00:02:42.952
At the Bastrop Texas plant, SpaceX added its own printed circuit board and panel level

00:02:42.952 --> 00:02:48.532
packaging lines, including a $280 million expansion announced in March 2025.

00:02:49.452 --> 00:02:53.652
Final assembly and tests came under one roof instead of boards routing through separate

00:02:53.652 --> 00:02:54.012
vendors.

00:02:54.632 --> 00:02:59.252
Each consolidation took out connectors, cut board layers, and shortened test time.

00:02:59.792 --> 00:03:02.572
The physics of electronic beam steering did not change.

00:03:03.012 --> 00:03:06.112
The number of physical interfaces changed, and so did the scale.

00:03:06.692 --> 00:03:11.252
One step moved the design from separate front-end modules to integrated dies that handled

00:03:11.252 --> 00:03:14.632
both amplification and phase shifting on the same substrate.

00:03:15.092 --> 00:03:19.732
That removed intermodule cabling and the impedance matching hardware that went with it.

00:03:20.192 --> 00:03:24.872
Another step put panel level packaging at ST microelectronics facilities in France,

00:03:25.472 --> 00:03:31.252
Malta, and Malaysia, so chips could be processed in larger formats before simulation, with fewer

00:03:31.252 --> 00:03:32.492
handling steps per die.

00:03:33.192 --> 00:03:38.332
SpaceX then brought circuit board population and terminal integration into Bastrop, so

00:03:38.332 --> 00:03:42.292
a board no longer traveled between vendors for each successive layer or test.

00:03:42.952 --> 00:03:44.032
The trade-off was real.

00:03:44.552 --> 00:03:48.532
The single-die and single-panel approach still had to meet the thermal and reliability

00:03:48.532 --> 00:03:52.832
targets that discrete modules had met with extra shielding and connectors.

00:03:53.452 --> 00:03:58.412
The engineering therefore went into the buy CMOS process itself, not into more external

00:03:58.412 --> 00:03:58.952
protection.

00:03:59.592 --> 00:04:04.872
Once those process improvements held at volume, production above 20,000 terminals a day let

00:04:04.872 --> 00:04:10.472
the capital cost of the Bastrop expansion spread across that output, and further automation became

00:04:10.472 --> 00:04:16.432
economical. Picture a consumer factory turning out a phased array, not a radar shop building one.

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:25.100
Manufacturing costs passed through $1,300 in early 2021, and later sat below the $599 sale price.

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:31.700
Jonathan Hauffeller credited the repeated production iterations for that change. By September 2023,

00:04:31.700 --> 00:04:37.460
the company no longer subsidized terminals. Limits remain. The buy-see-moss process depends

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:43.480
on a single European chip supplier, and the Bastrop line stay economical only a volume continues.

00:04:44.060 --> 00:04:47.920
Higher-end maritime and aviation terminals still carry different price points,

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:53.200
and those prices reflect added environmental hardening rather than the core beam-forming cost.

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:59.100
The chip flow from ST Microelectronics' European Fabs is now tight enough that a disruption

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:04.360
there would hit terminal output immediately. The in-house packaging lines also need sustained

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:09.820
demand to justify a three-year buildout of an additional 1 million square feet, so the cost

00:05:09.820 --> 00:05:16.058
reduction is tied to subscriber growth as much as to any one design change. What travels is a test

00:05:16.058 --> 00:05:21.958
you can run on the next product, not a verdict on this one. Ask which costs exist only because the

00:05:21.958 --> 00:05:27.478
buyer was too small to order the function as one piece and the factory as one roof. If the answer

00:05:27.478 --> 00:05:31.698
After is most of them, the physics can stay as it is, and the price can still move.

00:05:32.258 --> 00:05:36.078
That test has not been run on most flat panels still made in small lots.

00:05:36.658 --> 00:05:40.798
It starts the day a single buyer is willing to underwrite both the die and the line.

00:05:41.418 --> 00:05:45.698
Which other flat panel device now built in small lots will first cross that threshold?

00:05:46.378 --> 00:05:48.718
That's a wrap on today's first principles breakdown.

